matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139534152 17592186045417 false] [17592186115065 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/98/98b93ad401053c6e2be3d5899202b6de802d0b540406797a5285360ca23b6148.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186115066 1071 "Let ![](images/15/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](images/18/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" 13194139534152 17592186045417 false] [17592186115066 1071 "Let ![](images/2d/2de33a648364672e24ed14d9ceb606eeec07da3dc5e8ab1bdf4c00440f2229ac.svg) and ![](images/b3/b3ca8db3f26fed706e2786f2c7a09b39800bb249f72565039d7fe6493a813507.svg) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186115066 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/3a/3a82d0f5d2e89d80c3e5f541e205d3052264d967db9736d86de3f52418319a29.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186115066 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/43/43da43be5ab47c640387f77d3b61555d79e9b9a2d61f49d00b56e45a6856bdec.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139534152 17592186045417 false] [17592186115067 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/60/60966175c795cda23b462c4dc57bb694acbd0ec5ce3ffda29cb20fba41a86d60.svg), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186115067 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/db/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139534152 17592186045417 false] [17592186115235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412421 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186115236 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412420 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186115476 1050 420.0 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186116491 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." 13194139534152 17592186045417 false] [17592186116491 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412408 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412408 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412408 1083 #uuid "023017fb-407e-5bef-96fe-5ca4f9043072" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412409 1080 "I only" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412409 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412409 1083 #uuid "0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412410 1080 "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412410 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412410 1083 #uuid "236e5192-a40c-577c-b850-ee910be00f48" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412411 1080 "k=-1" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412411 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412411 1083 #uuid "2fd3908a-3f72-5df0-977b-949624bbea37" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412412 1080 "II only" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412412 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412412 1083 #uuid "578d705a-4c53-5798-b6ab-04cb9c9c2712" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412413 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412413 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412413 1083 #uuid "ad20df4f-b7fd-5955-aa00-9373ab0ec236" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412414 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412414 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412414 1083 #uuid "bffc8d55-ee5e-5f8f-9215-8f1f05d7db3b" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412415 1080 "III only" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412415 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412415 1083 #uuid "cb491f25-cc92-5958-9889-8d4238a0075e" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412416 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412416 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412416 1083 #uuid "de2cfeee-36d6-5198-8124-8b5f27baa495" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412417 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ is not invertible" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412417 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412417 1083 #uuid "fa69be2d-b4be-5af7-a14e-91a403fff329" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412408 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412409 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412412 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412415 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412416 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1077 "selection" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412409 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412418 1079 #uuid "6af571c7-96ee-5d86-b8e5-c8021a9767c5" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412410 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412411 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412413 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412414 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412417 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1077 "selection" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412410 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412419 1079 #uuid "bcbfa28f-c9f4-5861-a817-67e808342830" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412419 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412420 1069 "q-128146" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412420 1071 "Consider the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412420 1072 #uuid "12b35a91-483f-55c8-a96f-b8126a049cff" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412420 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nFinally, notice that if $k$ can't be equal to $-1,$ since  $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -(-1)\\end{aligned}]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*f the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0.$*" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412418 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412421 1069 "q-32169" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412421 1071 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The lines $5x-4y=0$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist\n- $Ax=0$ has no solution" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412421 1072 #uuid "79c14c2f-568d-5ff3-b628-6c8dd8d0639f" 13194139534152 17592186045417 true] [17592186412421 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of  the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." 13194139534152 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-023017fb-407e-5bef-96fe-5ca4f9043072" 17592186412408 "answer-0d7b32a8-3cec-559f-a17c-bf7b28c6fbce" 17592186412409 "answer-236e5192-a40c-577c-b850-ee910be00f48" 17592186412410 "answer-2fd3908a-3f72-5df0-977b-949624bbea37" 17592186412411 "answer-578d705a-4c53-5798-b6ab-04cb9c9c2712" 17592186412412 "answer-ad20df4f-b7fd-5955-aa00-9373ab0ec236" 17592186412413 "answer-bffc8d55-ee5e-5f8f-9215-8f1f05d7db3b" 17592186412414 "answer-cb491f25-cc92-5958-9889-8d4238a0075e" 17592186412415 "answer-de2cfeee-36d6-5198-8124-8b5f27baa495" 17592186412416 "answer-fa69be2d-b4be-5af7-a14e-91a403fff329" 17592186412417 "answer-field-6af571c7-96ee-5d86-b8e5-c8021a9767c5" 17592186412418 "answer-field-bcbfa28f-c9f4-5861-a817-67e808342830" 17592186412419 "question-12b35a91-483f-55c8-a96f-b8126a049cff" 17592186412420 "question-79c14c2f-568d-5ff3-b628-6c8dd8d0639f" 17592186412421}}
